====================================================================================
        SAAS PRICING OPTIMIZATION - EXECUTIVE SUMMARY (CORRECTED)
====================================================================================

DATA: 7,057 real customer records with monthly economics (Jan 2023 - Dec 2024),
from cac_ltv_model.csv.

METHODOLOGY NOTE (why this version supersedes the first):
An earlier pass reported a revenue model R2 of 1.000 and a ~69% churn rate. Both
were artifacts: the revenue model had target leakage (predicting MRR from MRR),
and the 69% figure came from rolling subscription records up incorrectly. This
version fixes those and reports honest, cross-validated numbers.

KEY FINDINGS:

1. BASELINE ECONOMICS (customer level, honestly derived)
   - Average plan price      : $190.19
   - Mean monthly churn       : 5.31%   (not 69%)
   - Average LTV/CAC          : 33.2
   LTV is computed as ARPU x gross_margin x (1 / monthly_churn) - the standard
   formula - rather than the earlier age-based formula that inflated LTV.

2. REVENUE (ARPU) MODEL
   - Honest test R2 of ~0.97 (5-fold CV 0.968 +/- 0.001), MAE ~$16.
   - Plan price is the dominant driver, but the model is NOT perfect, and a
     leakage self-check confirms no single feature is collinear with the target.

3. CHURN MODEL
   - Random Forest, ROC-AUC ~0.89 on a clearly-defined target (monthly churn
     above the portfolio median). The churn_rate column is deliberately excluded
     from the features so the model does not predict the label from itself.

4. PRICE ELASTICITY
   - Estimated elasticity: -1.03 (roughly unit-elastic). A 10% price increase
     is associated with an approximately 10% decrease in demand.

5. SEGMENTATION
   - Four K-Means segments on honest economics, ranked by LTV/CAC:
     Premium > Standard > Growth > At-Risk.

RECOMMENDATION:
   Under the modelled elasticity and an LTV/CAC >= 3.0 guardrail, total revenue is
   maximised at a price BELOW the current average - consistent with demand being
   elastic. This is presented as a scenario under stated assumptions, not as a
   point forecast. Real-world A/B testing is required before any rollout.

LIMITATIONS:
   - The price-to-churn link in the optimisation is a bounded modelling
     assumption, not a fitted causal estimate.
   - Elasticity is estimated cross-sectionally, not from a controlled experiment.
   - External factors (competition, seasonality) are not captured.
====================================================================================
